WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.549
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Kevin from Virginia,
Kevin from Virginia.

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:05.549
You're on the air.

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:07.759
Speaker 4: Hello, David.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:08.759
Hello.

00:00:08.759 --> 00:00:12.000
I realize this is kind of old news at this
point, but I've been trying to call it the

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.750
last couple of weeks to talk about permanent
daylight saving time.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:17.750
OK.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:23.400
But I am on on the side of Permanent Standard
Time and I could go on a diatribe.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.981
I personally think it's better, but I think
my best argument on the subject is more of

00:00:27.981 --> 00:00:29.260
a historical one.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.260
Hmm.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:37.000
So my framing of this issue is if if we are
going to go to permanent daylight saving time,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.249
but we're really doing is we're changing our
time zones by one time zone.

00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:45.700
And you're kind of saying if you want to go
to permanent daylight saving time, you're

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:51.420
saying we got it wrong when we established
the time zones in the late 80s.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.620
Or you think society has changed fundamentally
in that last hundred and fifty or so years,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.799
but we all benefit from shifting our schedules
by one hour?

00:01:02.799 --> 00:01:03.799
Sure.

00:01:03.799 --> 00:01:06.870
And I just don't think that's I don't think
that's accurate.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:12.311
I think it was a lot of thought put into the
time zones when they were established that

00:01:12.311 --> 00:01:17.200
it lined up with solar time that we used for
millennia.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.909
I don't think we should change that.

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:24.229
Speaker 1: So listen, I think that actually
society has changed dramatically since the

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:30.659
time zones were established by virtue of completely
ubiquitous electricity and artificial lighting,

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:31.659
I think.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:33.329
I think that it has changed now.

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:37.310
That's not reason alone to say, let's do permanent
daylight saving now.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.180
I lean towards permanent daylight saving.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.180
You know why?

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.200
Because I don't get up early enough to care
about it being dark in the morning, and I

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.070
get a lot of benefit from it being light out
an additional hour in winter in the northeast,

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:53.100
in middle of December.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.100
It's ugly.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.110
It's depressing what goes on in the northeast?

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:01.350
Now that being said, I am sensitive to the
fact that, you know, if you look at the western

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.479
edge of certain time zones, like, for example,
if you look at Columbus, Ohio is an interesting

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:11.780
place in Columbus, Ohio, there on the western
edge of the Eastern Time zone.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.830
If we were to do permanent daylight saving
time, if I understand correctly in December,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.780
sunrise would be something like eight 20 in
Columbus.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.030
I looked it up at one point I don't exactly
remember.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.510
I agree that that's kind of complicated.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.810
That's not so good.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:32.220
So another idea I heard was split the difference
make a 30 minute adjustment and make that

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:33.220
permanent.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:37.260
The problem is that then the difference between
our time zones and those in other parts of

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.000
the world are going to be, you know, six and
a half hours, eight and a half hours, which

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.540
is just very complicated.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.810
So I don't know that there's a perfect solution.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:52.030
I hate what we do now, and I lean towards
permanent daylight saving time.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.350
Speaker 4: I also think what we do now.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:03:01.840
Yeah, I personally, I think we need to be
taking a hard look at the schedule of the

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.350
business day schedule of schools.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:09.670
You know, there's been a lot written about
school starting too early and the health impacts

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:13.780
that has on our children are absolutely going
with permanent daylight saving time.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.480
We're kind of sidestepping the real issue.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.860
But yeah, the
Speaker 1: security issue also, which is there

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.560
are other countries where it's much, much
more typical that the business and work day

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.200
starts at either 9:30 or 10:00 and goes till
more like six or seven.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.840
And then, like in a lot of places in Europe,
you leave work at 7:00 and then you know,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.420
you go right to drinks and then a later dinner.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:37.550
Like when I was in Spain.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.100
Now Spain maintains the siesta for a lot of
workers.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.350
You open the store at 10:00 and then you're
off from one to three and then you're open

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.370
from three to seven.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:52.260
And at 7:00, you go for a drink and at 9:30
you have dinner and it's just a totally different

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.260
lifestyle.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.670
Now, culturally, I don't know that that will
work in the U.S., but there's many other configurations

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.520
of the daily schedule that could work.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.230
